=== L2 Material Removal: Cutting, Turning, Milling, Drilling ===
id: e4bde434-a540-40db-b52b-326cb4ed2709
slug: material-removal-cutting-turning-milling-drilling
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:54:53.464743+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 37

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Sawing, shearing, laser cutting, lathe turning, milling, drilling and PCB etching explained.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Is Subtractive Manufacturing?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Subtractive manufacturing starts with a block, sheet or bar of material and removes unwanted sections until the finished part remains. Every process in this lesson &mdash; sawing, shearing, laser cutting, turning, milling and drilling &mdash; is <dfn class="term" data-def="Any manufacturing process that removes material from a workpiece to achieve the desired shape, such as cutting, turning, milling or drilling.">subtractive</dfn>. The removed material becomes waste (swarf, chips or offcuts), so material selection and planning the cutting sequence carefully both reduce cost and waste.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Cutting: Sawing, Shearing and Laser Cutting</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">The simplest subtractive operations separate a workpiece into pieces. <strong>Sawing</strong> uses a toothed blade &mdash; a hacksaw in the workshop or a band saw on the factory floor &mdash; to cut through metal, timber or plastics. The teeth are set (bent alternately left and right) so the kerf (slot) is slightly wider than the blade body, preventing binding.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5"><strong>Shearing</strong> uses two hardened blades (like giant scissors) to cut sheet metal without a kerf. A hydraulic guillotine can shear steel plate up to 10&thinsp;mm thick cleanly and quickly. Because shearing requires no cutting fluid and produces no swarf, it is economical for straight-line cuts on sheet stock.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n6"><dfn class="term" data-def="A cutting process that uses a focused beam of coherent light to melt or vaporise material along a programmed path, cutting complex 2D profiles from sheet material.">Laser cutting</dfn> directs a high-power CO&#8322; or fibre laser beam onto the material surface. Controlled by CNC, it can cut intricate 2D profiles in metal, acrylic and timber with very narrow kerfs (as small as 0.1&thinsp;mm) and no physical tool contact. Laser cutting is ideal for prototype sheet-metal brackets and signage panels but is limited to thin sections &mdash; typically below 25&thinsp;mm for mild steel.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n7" data-revision-tip="Cover this and state: what is the kerf, and why does shearing produce no kerf or swarf?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Sawing produces a kerf (slot) and swarf; shearing produces a clean cut with no kerf and no swarf; laser cutting produces a very narrow kerf and is CNC-controlled, allowing complex profiles to be cut automatically.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">Turning on the Lathe</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A machine tool that rotates a workpiece on its axis while a stationary cutting tool is fed along or across the workpiece to remove material and produce cylindrical shapes.">lathe</dfn> rotates the workpiece while a single-point cutting tool is fed along or into the material. Key parts of the lathe that examiners test regularly are:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n10"><strong>Chuck</strong> &mdash; the rotating clamp at the headstock that grips the workpiece. A three-jaw self-centring chuck grips round or hexagonal bar automatically; a four-jaw independent chuck can grip irregular shapes.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n11"><strong>Tailstock</strong> &mdash; the adjustable support at the opposite end of the bed. It can hold a live centre to support long workpieces, or a drill chuck for drilling on the lathe&rsquo;s axis.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n12"><strong>Carriage / tool post</strong> &mdash; holds the cutting tool and moves along the bed (for parallel turning) or across (for facing).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n13"><strong>Bed</strong> &mdash; the precision-machined base that ensures the carriage moves parallel to the spindle axis.</li>
</ul>
<p data-narration-id="n14">Lathe operations include <strong>parallel (cylindrical) turning</strong> (reducing diameter along the length), <strong>facing</strong> (cutting across the end to produce a flat face), <strong>taper turning</strong> (angling the tool path to create a cone), and <strong>boring</strong> (enlarging an existing hole from the inside). Cutting speed and feed rate are set to suit the workpiece material &mdash; harder metals require slower speeds and a finer feed.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Calculating Spindle Speed from Cutting Speed</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n15">Every material has a recommended <strong>cutting speed</strong> (in metres per minute, m/min) &mdash; the speed at which the cutting tool moves through the material at the point of contact. For a rotating workpiece on a lathe, this depends on both the spindle speed (revolutions per minute, rpm) and the diameter of the workpiece.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n16">The relationship is:
    $$N = \frac{1000 \times v}{\pi \times d}$$
    where \(N\) = spindle speed (rpm), \(v\) = cutting speed (m/min), \(d\) = workpiece diameter (mm).</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n17"><strong>Worked example:</strong> A 40&thinsp;mm diameter mild steel bar is to be turned. The recommended cutting speed for mild steel is 30&thinsp;m/min. Find the required spindle speed.
    $$N = \frac{1000 \times 30}{\pi \times 40} = \frac{30\,000}{125.66} \approx 239 \text{ rpm}$$
    The lathe would be set to the nearest available speed below 239&thinsp;rpm to avoid over-speed and tool damage.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n18">Notice that a larger diameter requires a <em>lower</em> spindle speed to maintain the same cutting speed at the surface. If the diameter halves, the spindle speed must double. This inverse relationship is a common exam question.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n19">Milling</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n20"><dfn class="term" data-def="A subtractive process in which a rotating multi-tooth cutter removes material from the surface of a workpiece that is fed past the cutter, producing flat faces, slots, pockets and complex profiles.">Milling</dfn> uses a rotating multi-tooth cutter. Unlike a lathe (where the workpiece rotates), in milling the cutter rotates and the workpiece is clamped to a table that moves in X, Y and Z directions. Milling operations include:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n21"><strong>Face milling</strong> &mdash; a large-diameter cutter with many teeth sweeps across the top surface to produce a flat, smooth face. Used to true-up castings and forgings.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22"><strong>Slot milling</strong> &mdash; an end mill (a cutter with teeth on its end and sides) cuts a slot or keyway into the workpiece surface.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n23"><strong>Profile milling</strong> &mdash; the CNC table follows a programmed path to cut complex 2D or 3D shapes, used heavily in aerospace for aluminium wing ribs and in automotive for cylinder-head ports.</li>
</ul>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n24" data-revision-tip="Without looking, explain the key difference between a lathe and a milling machine in terms of what rotates.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>On a lathe the <em>workpiece</em> rotates and the tool is stationary (moves linearly). On a milling machine the <em>cutter</em> rotates and the workpiece is clamped to a moving table. Getting this the wrong way round is a common exam mistake.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n25">Drilling</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n26">A <strong>pillar drill</strong> (also called a drill press) holds a drill bit in a rotating chuck and feeds it vertically into the workpiece. Tool names examiners expect you to know:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n27"><strong>Centre drill</strong> &mdash; a short, rigid combined drill-and-countersink used first to create a precise starting point, preventing the twist drill from wandering.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n28"><strong>Twist drill</strong> &mdash; the standard helical drill for producing through-holes and blind holes. Flutes carry swarf out of the hole.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n29"><strong>Countersink</strong> &mdash; a conical cutter that bevels the top of a hole so a countersunk screw sits flush with the surface.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n30"><strong>Counterbore</strong> &mdash; a stepped cutter that enlarges the top of a hole to receive a cap-head bolt so its head sits below the surface.</li>
</ul>
<p data-narration-id="n31">The sequence for drilling a precision hole is: mark out the centre with a centre punch &rarr; use a centre drill to start the hole accurately &rarr; drill the through-hole with a twist drill at the correct speed &rarr; de-burr the hole edges. Giving &ldquo;drill&rdquo; alone in an answer is not specific enough; always state the type of drill bit.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Chemical Etching for PCB Manufacture</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n32"><dfn class="term" data-def="A chemical process that removes unwanted copper from a PCB blank by immersing it in ferric chloride solution, leaving the protected circuit tracks intact.">Chemical etching</dfn> is used to produce printed circuit boards (PCBs). The process is subtractive but uses chemistry rather than mechanical cutting. A blank PCB consists of a fibreglass substrate coated in a thin copper layer. The desired circuit pattern is printed onto the copper using UV-sensitive etch-resist film, which is exposed and developed. The board is then immersed in ferric chloride (iron(III) chloride) solution, which dissolves the unprotected copper while leaving the resist-covered tracks intact. After etching, the resist is removed, revealing the finished copper tracks. Chemical etching bridges the Manufacturing and Systems units because PCBs are the foundation of electronic circuits.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n34">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n35">Subtractive processes (sawing, shearing, laser cutting, turning, milling, drilling) all remove material from a workpiece &mdash; each suits different shapes, quantities and materials. Shearing has no kerf or swarf; laser cutting is CNC-controlled for complex 2D profiles.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n36">On a lathe the workpiece rotates; on a milling machine the cutter rotates. Lathe operations include parallel turning, facing, taper turning and boring. The spindle speed formula is \(N = \frac{1000v}{\pi d}\).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n37">For drilling: always name the specific bit (centre drill first, then twist drill). Chemical etching is the subtractive method for PCB production, using ferric chloride to dissolve unprotected copper.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n33">Exam questions on drilling often ask you to name specific drill types &mdash; &ldquo;drill&rdquo; alone scores zero. Always write &ldquo;centre drill&rdquo;, &ldquo;twist drill&rdquo; or &ldquo;countersink&rdquo;. For lathe-parts questions, make sure you can name and locate the chuck, tailstock, carriage and bed &mdash; half of students cannot describe the chuck or tailstock in detail. For spindle-speed calculations, show every step of working: write out the formula, substitute values with units, then calculate. Method marks are awarded even when the final answer is wrong, so setting out the formula correctly is worth marks on its own.</p>

