=== L5 Heat Treatment and Chemical Treatment ===
id: f2cd1f81-8abc-4f0b-8a4a-abc7b513d830
slug: heat-treatment-and-chemical-treatment
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:56:28.325022+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 27

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Normalising, annealing, hardening, quenching and case hardening — what each does to metal grain structure.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Heat-Treat Metals?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">The mechanical properties of a metal &mdash; its hardness, strength, ductility and toughness &mdash; are determined not only by its chemical composition but also by the size and arrangement of the crystals (grains) inside it. Heat treatment deliberately alters grain structure by controlling how a metal is heated and then cooled. This gives engineers the ability to adjust properties after manufacturing: soften a steel so it can be bent without cracking, or harden a cutting tool so it keeps its edge under use.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">All heat-treatment processes for carbon steel share three stages: heat to a <dfn class="term" data-def="The temperature at which the desired microstructural change occurs in the steel, typically in the range 700–900 °C for most carbon steels.">soaking temperature</dfn> appropriate for the process; hold at that temperature long enough for heat to penetrate the section (soaking); then cool at a controlled rate. The soaking temperature and cooling rate together determine which process you are performing and what properties result.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">Normalising</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5"><dfn class="term" data-def="A heat treatment in which steel is heated above its critical temperature and then cooled in still air, producing a uniform, fine grain structure and relieving internal stresses caused by previous manufacturing operations.">Normalising</dfn> restores a uniform grain structure after a manufacturing process that has distorted it. Forging, rolling, welding and cold working all introduce internal stresses and can produce a coarse or non-uniform grain. Normalising heats the steel above its upper critical temperature (typically 830&ndash;900&thinsp;&deg;C for medium-carbon steels) and then allows it to cool in still air. The still-air cooling rate is moderate &mdash; faster than furnace cooling, slower than water quenching &mdash; producing a fine, uniform <dfn class="term" data-def="The crystalline microstructure of a metal, made up of individual crystals called grains separated by grain boundaries; finer grain gives higher strength and toughness.">grain structure</dfn>. The result is a steel with improved toughness and consistent mechanical properties throughout the section.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Cover this and match each heat treatment to its purpose: normalising, annealing, hardening/quenching, tempering.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Four key processes, four distinct purposes: <strong>Normalise</strong> = restore uniform grain after work-hardening or welding. <strong>Anneal</strong> = fully soften for further cold working. <strong>Harden + quench</strong> = maximise hardness (but brittle). <strong>Temper</strong> = reduce brittleness after hardening. These must not be confused with one another.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Annealing</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8"><dfn class="term" data-def="A heat treatment in which steel is heated above its critical temperature and then cooled very slowly (usually inside the furnace with the power turned off) to produce a soft, ductile microstructure.">Annealing</dfn> is the process of making a metal as soft and ductile as possible. The steel is heated to above its upper critical temperature (same range as normalising) and then cooled <em>very slowly</em> &mdash; typically inside the furnace with the power switched off, taking many hours. This slow cooling gives atoms time to arrange themselves into large, coarse grains with very few dislocations, maximising ductility and making the metal easy to cold-work (bend, draw or stamp) without cracking.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9">Process annealing (also called sub-critical annealing or &ldquo;softening&rdquo;) is a variant used between stages of wire drawing or sheet rolling: the steel is heated just below the critical temperature and cooled slowly. This relieves the work-hardening that accumulates with each cold-working pass, allowing further deformation without fracture.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">Hardening</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11">Hardening makes carbon steel as hard as possible &mdash; but at the cost of making it brittle. The steel is heated above its upper critical temperature until it forms a uniform austenite structure throughout. It is then <dfn class="term" data-def="Rapid cooling of a heated steel by immersion in a liquid (water, oil or brine) to trap the hard martensite microstructure before the steel can return to its softer equilibrium structure.">quenched</dfn> (plunged rapidly into water, oil or brine). The sudden cooling prevents the atoms from reorganising into the soft structure they would normally form; instead, a highly stressed, needle-like microstructure called <strong>martensite</strong> is trapped. Martensite is extremely hard (the hardest microstructure in steel) but also very brittle &mdash; a hardened steel file will shatter rather than bend.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n12">The choice of quench medium affects the cooling rate: water quenches fastest (maximum hardness, maximum distortion risk); oil is slower (slightly lower hardness, less distortion); brine (salt water) quenches fastest of all. For high-alloy steels, even air quenching may be fast enough to achieve full hardness.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Tempering &mdash; Reducing Brittleness After Hardening</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n13">A fully hardened steel is too brittle for most engineering applications &mdash; it would crack under impact. Tempering is always carried out after hardening to reduce brittleness while retaining as much hardness as possible. The hardened steel is reheated to a temperature <em>below</em> the lower critical temperature (typically 150&ndash;600&thinsp;&deg;C), held for a set time, then cooled in air. The heat allows some of the trapped martensite to transform to a tougher structure, releasing internal stresses.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n14">The higher the tempering temperature, the tougher (and softer) the result. Colours appearing on a polished steel surface during tempering indicate the temperature reached: straw (230&thinsp;&deg;C) for woodworking chisels; blue (300&thinsp;&deg;C) for springs and screwdrivers; dark blue for saws. This temper colour method is a traditional way for a blacksmith to judge the tempering temperature without a thermometer.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n15">Case Hardening</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n16"><dfn class="term" data-def="A surface-hardening process in which a low-carbon steel component is heated in a carbon-rich atmosphere, allowing carbon to diffuse into the outer layer; the component is then quenched to harden only the surface while the tough, soft core is retained.">Case hardening</dfn> is used when an engineer needs a component with a hard, wear-resistant surface and a tough, ductile core that resists impact. Gear teeth and camshaft lobes are classic examples: the surface must resist wear, but the core must resist the bending forces that would shatter a fully hardened component. Low-carbon steel (which normally cannot be hardened by quenching because it lacks sufficient carbon) is packed in a carbon-rich compound and heated in a furnace. Carbon diffuses into the outer layer over hours, raising the carbon content of the case to levels that allow hardening. The component is then quenched, hardening the carbon-enriched case while leaving the core (still low carbon) tough and ductile.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n17" data-revision-tip="Close this and explain in two sentences why a gear tooth needs case hardening rather than full through-hardening.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Case hardening gives a hard surface (to resist wear and pitting from contact forces) and a tough core (to resist the bending loads without shattering). Full through-hardening would make the gear tooth too brittle to withstand bending &mdash; it would snap rather than deform.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Comparing the Heat Treatment Processes</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n18"><strong>Normalising:</strong> heat above critical temp &rarr; cool in still air &rarr; result: uniform fine grain, relieved stresses, moderate improvement in toughness.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n19"><strong>Annealing:</strong> heat above critical temp &rarr; cool very slowly in furnace &rarr; result: maximum ductility, coarse grain, softened for further cold working.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n20"><strong>Hardening:</strong> heat above critical temp &rarr; quench rapidly in water/oil &rarr; result: maximum hardness (martensite) but brittle.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n21"><strong>Tempering:</strong> reheat hardened steel below critical temp &rarr; cool in air &rarr; result: reduced brittleness, some hardness sacrificed for toughness.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n22"><strong>Case hardening:</strong> heat low-carbon steel in carbon-rich atmosphere &rarr; carbon diffuses into surface layer &rarr; quench &rarr; result: hard case + tough core.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n24">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n25">Normalising (cool in still air) restores a uniform fine grain after work-hardening or welding. Annealing (cool slowly in furnace) gives maximum ductility for cold working.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n26">Hardening (heat then quench rapidly) produces hard but brittle martensite. Tempering (reheat below critical temp after hardening) reduces brittleness while retaining much of the hardness.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n27">Case hardening gives low-carbon steel a hard wear-resistant surface and a tough ductile core &mdash; essential for gear teeth, camshafts and other components subject to both surface wear and bending loads.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n23">The most common confusion is between normalising and annealing: both heat to above the critical temperature, but annealing cools inside the furnace (very slowly &rarr; maximum ductility) while normalising cools in still air (moderate rate &rarr; fine grain, not maximum softness). The second common confusion is hardening and quenching &mdash; these are one combined process, not two alternatives: harden (heat) then quench (cool rapidly) together produce martensite. If an exam question asks &ldquo;what is the purpose of tempering?&rdquo;, always include that it follows hardening and reduces brittleness by partially converting martensite to a tougher structure.</p>

