+    "path": "[5].marks",
+    "expect": "Award marks for developed comparative points. Up to 6 marks across the four areas:\n\nMaterials: SLA uses liquid photopolymer resin; metal sintering uses fine metal powder (e.g. stainless steel, titanium, aluminium alloy).\n\nEnergy source: SLA uses an ultraviolet laser to cure/harden the resin; metal sintering uses a high-powered infrared laser to fuse the powder particles.\n\nSurface finish: SLA produces a very smooth surface finish with fine detail, better than FDM; metal sintering produces a slightly rough surface (powder granules) but close to the finish of machined metal.\n\nTypical application: SLA — appearance prototypes, dental models, jewellery; metal sintering — aerospace turbine components, medical implants, lightweight structural brackets.\n\n[6 marks — must compare, not just describe each in isolation. 1 mark per simple point, 2 marks for a developed comparative point. Max 6.]",
+    "value": "Award marks for developed comparative points. Up to 6 marks across the four areas:\n\nMaterials: SLA uses liquid photopolymer resin; metal sintering uses fine metal powder (e.g. stainless steel, titanium, aluminium alloy).\n\nEnergy source: SLA uses an ultraviolet laser to cure/harden the resin; metal sintering uses a high-powered infrared laser to fuse the powder particles.\n\nSurface finish: SLA produces a very smooth surface finish with fine detail, better than FDM; metal sintering produces a rough surface with powder particles and may need machining or polishing where a smooth finish is required.\n\nTypical application: SLA — appearance prototypes, dental models, jewellery; metal sintering — aerospace turbine components, medical implants, lightweight structural brackets.\n\n[6 marks — must compare, not just describe each in isolation. 1 mark per simple point, 2 marks for a developed comparative point. Max 6.]",
