+    "lesson": 5,
+    "field": "practice_questions",
+    "path": "[5].marks",
+    "expect": "Mastering (7-8 marks): Full hardening-and-tempering sequence described with reasons for each stage linked to the service requirements of a twist drill (must cut mild steel — needs hardness; must not shatter under drilling loads — needs toughness). Tempering temperature range discussed.\n\nSecure (5-6 marks): Hardening and tempering described. Some link to drill requirements. Reasoning present but may miss tempering temperature detail.\n\nDeveloping (3-4 marks): Hardening described; tempering mentioned but not developed. Limited link to service requirements.\n\nEmerging (1-2 marks): Generic heat treatment points with no clear sequence or link to scenario.\n\nContent that may be credited:\n1. Hardening: heat the high-speed steel twist drill above its upper critical temperature; hold to soak uniformly; quench in oil (high-speed steel is oil-hardened) to produce a hard martensitic structure.\n2. Reason: martensite gives the hardness needed to cut mild steel without the cutting edges wearing or deforming.\n3. Tempering: reheat the hardened drill to 150–250 °C (straw colour on polished surface), hold, then cool in air.\n4. Reason: a fully hardened drill would be too brittle — the flutes would snap under the torque of drilling. Tempering converts some martensite to a tougher structure, improving toughness without significantly reducing hardness.\n5. Result: the finished drill has sufficient hardness to cut mild steel and sufficient toughness to withstand the drilling loads in service.",
+    "value": "High-speed steel needs a grade-specific heat-treatment schedule. Credit answers that:\n- identify the exact high-speed-steel grade and follow its manufacturer’s heat-treatment data\n- use the specified preheating and austenitising stages, holding times and cooling method\n- temper at the grade’s stated high-speed-steel temperature, normally in repeated cycles\n- explain that hardening provides wear resistance and tempering provides sufficient toughness\n\nDo not credit a 150–250 °C straw temper as the standard treatment for high-speed steel; it is not a general high-speed-steel schedule.",
